insert into public.marking_rubrics (key, department, title, body, source_url, source_note) values
($r$ucl-philosophy$r$,$r$Philosophy$r$,$r$UCL Philosophy marking criteria$r$,$r$Use for Philosophy essays. Mark against four qualities: use of reading, argumentation, quality of writing, and engagement with the question, then form an overall judgement. Classification bands: 70+ First, 60-69 Upper Second, 50-59 Lower Second, 40-49 Third, below 40 Fail. The department's grid tops out at 80; marks above 80 are uncommon (especially in technical/logic modules, where module-specific criteria apply instead).

70-80 (First): Reading is extensive, goes beyond the core list, and illustrative material is used to the point. The argument is clear and rigorous and shows real insight or critical ability. Writing is very well organised and very clear, each paragraph leading naturally to the next. The question is tackled head-on and the response is framed in fresh, relevant ways. The work need not be flawless but should show sophistication, originality or sound judgement, or exceptional clarity. Reserve 75+ for work that plainly grasps the complexity of the issues and is distinctively excellent.

60-69 (Upper Second): Reading is relevant but limited, with little sign of wider reading. Good analysis and critical thinking with a firm grasp of the concepts. Well organised, clearly expressed, and answers the question directly and relevantly. A well-structured, well-argued piece that shows good command of the subject, though it may miss parts of the question, have weaker patches, or hold back the author's own view.

50-59 (Lower Second): Little sign of close reading. Competently restates ideas from lectures or textbooks with little independent critical assessment. Organisation competent, expression moderately clear. Shows a general understanding of the question but does not keep referring back to it; material mostly relevant but poorly integrated. Sound grasp of basic facts and arguments, but these are not placed in an analytical framework and some key aspects are missed.

40-49 (Third): Only a very general acquaintance with the field; claims are asserted without support and the approach is entirely uncritical. Poorly organised and hard to follow. Some understanding of the question and topic, but many key aspects are not covered.

Below 40 (Fail): Errors or incoherence showing the basic course material has not been grasped; serious mistakes or misunderstandings. Little or no organisation (e.g. isolated points in note form). Material is irrelevant or the question is not answered. At most some evidence of basic note-taking or textbook reading.$r$,$r$https://www.ucl.ac.uk/philosophy/sites/philosophy/files/marking_grid_2.pdf$r$,$r$Paraphrased from the UCL Philosophy Department marking grid (PDF, undated) together with the department's 'Marking Criteria' page (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/arts-humanities/philosophy/current-students/marking-criteria), which names the four essay criteria and the BA bands. The grid is undated and the PDF's column layout was partly ambiguous, so the 50-59/below-40 placement of 'serious mistakes' is a best reading. A newer 'UCL Philosophy Department, Undergraduate Marking Grid' (PHIL_markingUG.pdf, uploaded 2025-09) exists but could not be opened; check it against this entry.$r$),
($r$ucl-geography$r$,$r$Geography$r$,$r$UCL Geography marking criteria$r$,$r$Use for Geography coursework essays. Judge the answer on seven criteria: (1) response to the task - argument, structure and conclusions; (2) grasp of the relevant issues; (3) engagement with the literature; (4) analysis - reflection, thought and conceptual framework; (5) empirical knowledge and use of examples; (6) writing and communication; (7) visual material and referencing. Place the work in the band where most criteria sit at that level or above. Criteria 6 and 7 then act as adjustments of up to +/-3 marks.

90+ (Exceptional): sophisticated argument with precise, subtle conclusions; exceptional grasp of the complexity and significance of the issues; literature review of expert, publishable quality; analysis aware of cutting-edge work within a sophisticated framework; comprehensive, precise evidence with critical reflection on the data. Writing reads as if professionally edited (+3); presentation and referencing exceptional (+3).
80-89 (Outstanding): nuanced, relevant argument and insightful conclusions; striking grasp of the issues; ambitious reading understood impressively; insightful analysis using concepts accurately in context; detailed, distinctive evidence. Writing with verve (+2); visuals actively advance the argument (+2).
70-79 (Excellent): distinctive response with clear argument, sensible and nuanced conclusions; thorough grasp with some sophisticated insight; extensive reading thoroughly understood; innovative analysis with concepts defined deftly in theoretical context; detailed, highly relevant evidence for most claims. Fluent writing (+1); flawless visuals and references (+1).
60-69 (Good): sound response and reasonable argument with logical conclusions; sound understanding including wider implications; plenty of relevant reading soundly understood; the student's own analysis with concepts used systematically; substantial good-quality evidence for most claims. Writing and visuals rarely detract (0).
50-59 (Fair): reasonable response but limited argument and partial conclusions; reasonable grasp; relevant reading with some understanding; reproduces taught ideas reasonably, rudimentary use of concepts; some evidence, not always used well. Writing/visuals sometimes detract (-1 each).
40-49 (Adequate): indirect response that only gestures at a relevant argument; rudimentary, patchy grasp with confusions; major gaps in reading and weak understanding; partial reproduction of ideas, concepts missing or misused; little evidence, claims rarely supported. Writing/visuals seriously detract (-2 each).
Below 40 (Inadequate): no argument or an irrelevant one; misunderstanding of the issues; very limited or irrelevant reading; erroneous analysis without conceptual content; evidence absent, irrelevant or wrong. 30-39 if three criteria are at this level, 20-29 if four, 1-19 if five. Writing that obstructs comprehension and poor formatting/referencing each cost -3.$r$,$r$https://www.ucl.ac.uk/geography/sites/geography/files/ug_criteria_october_2017.pdf$r$,$r$Paraphrased from UCL Geography 'Marking Criteria for Coursework' (file named ug_criteria_october_2017.pdf; the page itself shows no date). Likely dates from October 2017, so may be superseded. The same document also has separate criteria for exams, dissertations, posters, presentations and lab/field reports not summarised here. A 'Grade Descriptors and Marking Criteria' page on geog.ucl.ac.uk also exists but could not be fetched (robots-blocked).$r$),
($r$ucl-archaeology$r$,$r$Institute of Archaeology$r$,$r$UCL Archaeology marking criteria$r$,$r$Use for Institute of Archaeology undergraduate coursework (the same rubric is used across task types; skip criteria such as visuals if not relevant). Bands: Outstanding 75+, Excellent 70-75, Good 60-69, Fair 50-59, Adequate 40-49, Inadequate below 40. Threshold rule: if a threshold criterion (academic integrity, e.g. no usable references or bibliography, or unacknowledged/very inappropriate use of software) is failed, the work cannot pass, whatever its other merits. Expectations rise by year: Year 2 top bands expect insight and research beyond what was taught and weighing of sources; Year 3/4 top bands also expect originality, ambitious reading and clear discrimination between sources.

Argument: 75+ convincing, very clearly structured, compelling and weighs competing views; 70-75 sound, clearly structured, logical with straightforward conclusions; 60-69 reasonable and on the question but structure/argument/conclusions could improve; 50-59 answers directly but needs significant work to go beyond general understanding; 40-49 indirect, only gestures at a relevant argument; below 40 does not address the question.
Knowledge: 75+ grasps the complexities; 70-75 sound with some sense of wider implications; 60-69 good with minor slips; 50-59 repeated slips; 40-49 rudimentary, confused on non-core points; below 40 misunderstands core material.
Evidence (including choice of case studies): 75+ every claim supported, almost no room to improve; 70-75 all claims supported, little to improve; 60-69 some unsupported claims or detail/relevance could improve; 50-59 some unsupported claims and much to improve; 40-49 evidence attempted but weak or poorly used; below 40 no real support, or irrelevant/inaccurate evidence.
Analysis: 75+ the student's own analysis with clearly defined, systematically used concepts; 70-75 some original analysis or excellent reproduction of taught ideas; 60-69 good reproduction, most concepts defined; 50-59 reasonable reproduction, rudimentary concepts; 40-49 limited, concepts undefined or misused; below 40 wrong or absent.
Sources: 75+ wide engagement with recommended reading plus some beyond it; 70-75 thorough engagement with recommended sources; 60-69 good use of the essential sources; 50-59 some relevant sources; 40-49 limited or weak understanding; below 40 no relevant reading.
Referencing, visuals and writing: 75+ flawless referencing, visuals that advance the argument, precise and wide vocabulary; down to below 40 with missing or unusable references, absent or irrelevant visuals, and errors that block comprehension.$r$,$r$https://www.ucl.ac.uk/social-historical-sciences/sites/social_historical_sciences/files/ucl_ioa_ug_marking_criteria_2024-25_v4.pdf$r$,$r$Paraphrased from UCL Institute of Archaeology undergraduate marking criteria, 2024/25 (v4). Year 1 rubric summarised in detail; Year 2 and 3/4 differences noted briefly. The source lists 70 in two bands, so the 70/75 boundary is approximate.$r$),
($r$ucl-statistical-science$r$,$r$Statistical Science$r$,$r$UCL Statistical Science marking criteria$r$,$r$Use for Statistical Science answers of any kind (exam questions, problem sheets, coursework, reports). The department uses one set of letter-grade descriptors for all assessed work; examiners decide which grade's criteria a script meets, and raw scores falling between grade boundaries are converted by linear interpolation. For technical answers, judge understanding of the question and material, accuracy of calculations and algebraic manipulation, soundness of the logical argument, and clarity of presentation; for discursive parts, also depth of discussion, insight and relevant reading.

80+ (A+): Satisfies everything required for an A, and is exceptional on at least two of: understanding, insight, depth of analysis, clarity of discussion, plus evidence of relevant knowledge or reading where the task calls for it.

70-79 (A): Satisfies everything required for a B and in addition shows at least one of: answers of consistently high quality, very secure understanding, a thorough discussion, clear expression, or signs of insight or wide reading. Small slips are acceptable; serious errors are not.

60-69 (B): Good grasp of what the question asks and of the main subject areas, with good technical skill - carrying out calculations and manipulations correctly and building a logical argument. Towards the top of the band, presentation should be clear. A few mistakes are fine provided none is serious.

50-59 (C): Reasonable understanding and reasonable skill, but falls short of B - for example because it only covers a narrow range of the material, or because gaps in technique lead to frequent mistakes or untidy, unclear presentation.

40-49 (D): Basic, limited understanding and some basic skill. There may be many mistakes, but the answer must show clear evidence of relevant knowledge.

Below 40 (F, fail): Not of pass standard - little relevant knowledge shown, with many errors, irrelevant material or misunderstandings. A blank or minimal attempt scores essentially zero.

Individual assessments may also carry their own rubrics and word limits (over-length reports may lose marks); follow those where given.$r$,$r$https://ucl.ac.uk/statistics/sites/statistics/files/ughb_2425.pdf$r$,$r$Paraphrased from the UCL Department of Statistical Science Undergraduate Student Handbook 2024/25 (Sept 2024), grade descriptors in 'Feedback on student work' (p.16) and the Assessment/Moderation sections. A 2026-27 handbook exists but could not be opened.$r$),
($r$ucl-information-studies$r$,$r$Information Studies$r$,$r$UCL Information Studies marking criteria$r$,$r$Use for Information Studies (DIS) undergraduate coursework. The department describes each class band in terms of mastery of the subject matter, how far the assignment's objectives are covered, the depth of analysis and critical reflection, independence from course materials, technical competence, and accuracy/expression. 40% is the pass mark. The department publishes no separate 80+ descriptor, so reserve the upper First range for work that shows the 70+ qualities to an exceptional degree.

70+ (First): Excellent work showing very high mastery of the subject matter. Analysis is mature, with critical reflection where the task calls for it. Objectives are fully met, and errors are few and minor.

60-69 (Upper Second): Good work with a sound, thorough grasp of the subject. Covers most of the assignment's objectives, makes a good attempt at analysis, and is generally technically competent, though there are some gaps.

50-59 (Lower Second): A fair piece that meets only the basic requirements. Analysis is superficial and leans heavily on course materials, with little critical reflection.

40-49 (Third): Poor work. Shows some familiarity with the subject but has major gaps and serious misconceptions. Only some of the basic requirements are met and technical competence is low.

Below 40 (Fail): The student has not engaged seriously with the subject and cannot handle questions that go beyond familiar material. Understanding, expression and structure are all seriously inadequate.$r$,$r$https://www.ucl.ac.uk/arts-humanities/information-studies/study/useful-information-undergraduate-students$r$,$r$Paraphrased from the UCL Department of Information Studies page 'Useful Information for Undergraduate Students', section 'The Meaning of Coursework Grades and Marks' (no date shown). The fuller DIS Student Handbook is mentioned there but not publicly linked.$r$),
($r$ucl-mathematics$r$,$r$Mathematics$r$,$r$UCL Mathematics marking criteria$r$,$r$Use for Mathematics exam questions, problem sheets and in-class tests (exams and midsessionals are all judged against the same letter-grade bands). For problem answers, correctness is what matters most: unjustified answers and poor exposition lose marks, and partial credit is given for genuine progress towards a solution.

80+ (A+): Meets every requirement for an A and is exceptional on at least two of: understanding, insight, depth of analysis, clarity of discussion, with relevant knowledge shown where appropriate.

70-79 (A): Meets every requirement for a B and also shows one or more of: high-quality answers across a wide range of questions, very secure understanding, a thorough and clearly expressed discussion, insight, or wide knowledge. Minor slips or inconsistencies are acceptable but there are no serious errors of knowledge or understanding.

60-69 (B): Good understanding of the questions and of the main parts of the subject, with good skill in calculation, algebraic manipulation and logical argument. Higher in the band, the working is clearly expressed and presented. A few mistakes are fine provided none is serious.

50-59 (C): Reasonable understanding and skill, but short of B - for example because too narrow a range of questions is tackled (some omitted or answered inappropriately), or because weaknesses in technique cause frequent errors or poor presentation.

40-49 (D): Basic but limited understanding and some basic skill. Many mistakes may be present, but there must be clear evidence of relevant knowledge.

Below 40 (F, fail): Not of pass standard. Near the top of the range, very limited understanding and little relevant knowledge, with many mistakes, irrelevant material or misunderstandings; at the bottom, little or no understanding of the questions or the subject.

Other task types: history-of-maths essays are judged on understanding primary and secondary sources, placing the mathematics in context, assessing its significance, and supporting conclusions with evidence and references (structure and clarity expected; grammar and style not penalised). Computing projects require code that correctly implements the intended algorithm and gives accurate output. Projects are judged on understanding, difficulty, quality of mathematical writing, literature review, originality, correctness and independence.$r$,$r$https://www.ucl.ac.uk/mathematical-physical-sciences/sites/mathematical_physical_sciences/files/marking-criteria.pdf$r$,$r$Paraphrased from the UCL Department of Mathematics 'Marking criteria' PDF (undated; it links to 2018-19 presentation guidelines, so may date from around 2018-19).$r$),
($r$ucl-computer-science$r$,$r$Computer Science$r$,$r$UCL Computer Science marking criteria$r$,$r$Use for Computer Science coursework, reports and project write-ups (BSc and MEng). Assess against the criteria that apply to the task: (1) quality of response to the task - answer, structure, conclusions; (2) understanding of the relevant issues; (3) engagement with related work, literature and existing solutions; (4) analysis - reflection, discussion, limitations; (5) the algorithm or technical solution; (6) testing - correctness, performance, evaluation; (7) presentation or demo; (8) writing, communication and documentation; (9) formatting, visuals, clarity and referencing. Note: for MEng students the 40-49 band is a fail, for BSc it is a Third.

90+ (exceptional First): exceptional response with precise conclusions and full grasp of the complexities; expert-level literature review with innovative synthesis; advanced, creative solution; exceptionally comprehensive testing; flawless, polished writing and presentation.
80-89 (outstanding First): convincing, sophisticated response; strong command of the issues and literature; advanced solution and very thorough testing; highly polished communication.
70-79 (First): distinctive response with a clear argument, sensible and nuanced conclusions; some sophisticated insight; reading engaged critically; a skilful, possibly novel algorithm or solution; testing very well done; persuasive delivery; fluent writing and impeccable formatting.
60-69 (Upper Second; 65+ high): sound response with logical argument and straightforward conclusions; understanding reaches wider implications; the student's own analysis with concepts used systematically; good solution with only minor faults; solid testing and evaluation; consistent, well-documented writing and formatting.
50-59 (Lower Second; 55+ high): reasonable response but limited argument and partial conclusions; reasonable understanding; relevant but thin reading; the solution works but has several implementation flaws; testing is basic and misses important cases; communication adequate but sometimes distracting.
40-49 (Third for BSc, fail for MEng): indirect, partly argued response; rudimentary and patchy understanding; gaps in reading; analysis only partly reproduces taught ideas; solution largely incomplete; minimal testing; weak communication and formatting.
Below 40 (Fail): task misread or no relevant argument; issues misunderstood; analysis wrong; solution missing or badly wrong; no testing; poor writing and presentation. 1-19 where the assignment has been misunderstood; 20-39 where the work is inadequate across the criteria.$r$,$r$http://www.cs.ucl.ac.uk/fileadmin/UCL-CS/students/documents/COMPS_ENG_Grade_Descriptor_1819.pdf$r$,$r$Paraphrased from 'UCL Computer Science: Marking Criteria and Grade Descriptors' (file named COMPS_ENG_Grade_Descriptor_1819, i.e. 2018-19 - may be superseded). Hosted on the department's own cs.ucl.ac.uk site. Band labels in the source table were not perfectly aligned, so boundaries follow the score ranges. Not intended for closed-book exam answers.$r$),
($r$ucl-biosciences$r$,$r$Division of Biosciences$r$,$r$UCL Biosciences (Life Sciences) marking criteria$r$,$r$Use for Biosciences / Life Sciences written answers (exam essays, short essays, coursework). The faculty scheme judges how much relevant, correct material is given, whether there are significant factual errors, how well the answer is organised and argued, and whether it engages critically with the evidence. Pass mark is 40 for BSc-level modules and 50 for Masters-level (MSci/M-level) modules.

91-100 (exceptional): Outstanding work of a standard that could be published as it stands.
81-90 (high First): Excellent, original work showing deep and critical understanding; potentially publishable.
76-80 (mid First): Unmistakably first-class: covers nearly everything relevant, discusses the evidence critically, is well argued and contains no significant errors.
70-75 (low First): A very good, correct answer showing insight and written well.
60-69 (Upper Second): Well organised and clearly expressed, with a good amount of correct factual content and no significant errors, but without the critical insight expected of a First.
50-59 (Lower Second): Good enough to pass but short on detail or argument, with one or two significant errors; shows the potential to do better. (50-55 is the lowest pass for Masters-level modules.)
40-49 (Third): Enough relevant points to pass at BSc level, but presentation is muddled, there are several significant errors, and judgement about what is important is weak. (46-49 fails at Masters level.)
35-39 (marginal fail): Addresses the question but with inadequate information and several significant errors; a little more work would have reached a BSc pass.
25-34 (fail): Weak knowledge throughout, many significant errors, poor presentation.
15-24 (clear fail): Does not really answer the question and shows little understanding; only two or three relevant points.
1-14 (weak fail): Far too brief or off the question; a single relevant point.
0: Irrelevant, unintelligible or no answer.$r$,$r$https://www.ucl.ac.uk/life-sciences/sites/life_sciences/files/study-undergraduate-neuroscience-ibsc-handbook-17-18.pdf$r$,$r$Paraphrased from the 'Harmonised Life Sciences Faculty Marking Scheme' (November 2015) as printed in the UCL Undergraduate Neuroscience iBSc Handbook 2017/18. Faculty-wide (the Biosciences division's own handbook is on Moodle only). Old (2015/2017) and may be superseded. The source gives the same descriptor for all of 60-69, so the 2:1 band is not split further.$r$)
on conflict (key) do update set department=excluded.department, title=excluded.title, body=excluded.body, source_url=excluded.source_url, source_note=excluded.source_note, updated_at=now();
select key, department from public.marking_rubrics order by key;